※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4400

※ `@[unique]` says there is one of a value of this type and it is never copied.
※
※ What it is for is a value that stands for something outside the program.  Two of those
※ would be two names for one thing, which is what the rule about a second `&mut` already
※ refuses for a reference and what nothing refused for the value itself.
※
※ **Reading a name as a value is what a copy is**, and that is the one place the rule has to
※ be checked: a field of such a value is read from where the value is, and a reference to it
※ is taken of that place.  Neither is a copy, and both are things a program may do -- so what
※ is refused is only having the whole of it somewhere else.

@[unique]
type Handle = fd : i32

fn peek(h: &Handle) → i32:
    h⌖.fd

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    let h: Handle = Handle(.fd ← 1i32)
    ※ A field of it, and a reference to it: neither is a copy.
    let n: i32 = h.fd + peek(&h)
    ※ And this is.
    let other: Handle = h
    ⎕narrow(n + other.fd, ⌜u6⌝) ?? 1u6
